The built-in assistant module (k109) keeps the whole conversation in its history and sends it with every request. The core refuses more than CANLIN_LLM_MESSAGES_MAX messages, and it refuses visibly - the request fails instead of the conversation being shortened. A long conversation therefore breaks rather than degrades. Decide what a turn drops: the oldest user/assistant pairs, a summary, or a bounded window - and put the rule in the llm-ui design §4 next to the other conversation rules. Found by the 2026-11-14 work on k109.

Rule written into docs/superpowers/specs/2026-09-22-canlin-llm-ui-design.md section 4, lines 121-136 (one paragraph plus its list, right after the 'Dropped and cut-off answers' rule), in the house pattern: bold lead, (decided 2026-11-15, dispatcher, k113), then the rule.

Facts checked in the code: CANLIN_LLM_MESSAGES_MAX = 256u in include/canlin/llm_limits.h:37; enforced in src/llm/llm.c:1202 (l_request, 'message count out of range' with code 'invalid'). The built-in assistant module (src/app/assistant.c, the ASSISTANT_SRC Lua) keeps the whole normalised conversation in one 'history' table and hands it to canlin.llm.request unchanged; it does NOT trim today. The implementation (llm lane, a card that follows) still has to come - the rule is written as the rule.

Two points for the maintainer: (1) the turn definition names the tool results of an answer as part of the turn, because the history holds role='tool' messages too and an orphan tool message would break the provider request - this is my clarification, the dispatcher's decision only named the user message and the assistant's answer. (2) Seam with providers spec 2.4 ('a history that no longer fits is refused, not shortened'): the new rule shortens on the message count, the byte budget stays a refusal; the paragraph says so in the text. The German literal ('Anfang des Gespraechs verworfen') is my wording, matching the module's note lines; the llm lane may adjust it.

